{"parentUuid":"cab550c8-ab2c-4345-80d3-9db30a620b23","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a0fe0e1e-23b8-44fc-8722-a9e008460227","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014kanAUS4M2NiDDmKSfKv5Y","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"summary\": \"Adversarial audit of the new search-in-TUI + `entire brain add` + prior-fix regressions\",\n3\t  \"agentCount\": 9,\n4\t  \"logs\": [\n5\t    \"v2 audit: 3 dimensions over search + add + prior-fix regressions\",\n6\t    \"raised 6, confirmed 4\"\n7\t  ],\n8\t  \"result\": {\n9\t    \"confirmed\": [\n10\t      {\n11\t        \"title\": \"A committed filter from the prior tab silently hides in-dashboard search results\",\n12\t        \"file\": \"internal/tui/tui.go\",\n13\t        \"line\": \"125-142\",\n14\t        \"severity\": \"bug\",\n15\t        \"detail\": \"The searchResultMsg handler switches to TabSearch and calls rebuildVisible() but never clears m.filter, unlike switchTab() (line 213) which does m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\"). A filter is committed (not just typed) by pressing Enter in updateFiltering (lines 264-267), which sets m.filtering=false while deliberately keeping m.filter.Value() so the filter stays applied. Because the main key switch only gates / and s behind !m.filtering (lines 147-152), the user can legitimately reach search with a non-empty committed filter. Sequence: on Facts, press '/', type a string, Enter (filter committed); then press 's', type a query, Enter. When searchResultMsg lands, rebuildVisible() (line 141 -> line 398) applies q = m.filter.Value() over the TabSearch results via filterKey(TabSearch, i), silently dropping any search hit that doesn't also contain the stale filter text. The tab bar still shows Search (N) from m.snap.count(TabSearch) (the unfiltered total, tui.go:473 / viewmodels.go:210-211), so the count and the visible list disagree and real results vanish. I confirmed this with a focused test: 2 search results existed, a stale committed filter 'zzznomatch' made visible=0 while the tab bar reported Search (2).\",\n16\t        \"suggested_fix\": \"In the searchResultMsg case (around line 136-141), clear the carried-over filter before rebuilding, mirroring switchTab: add m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\") (and, for consistency, m.filtering = false is already implied) immediately before m.rebuildVisible() so search results are shown unfiltered and the Search (N) count matches the visible rows.\",\n17\t        \"dimension\": \"search-fsm\",\n18\t        \"verdict\": {\n19\t          \"real\": true,\n20\t          \"reasoning\": \"Confirmed by reading the code. The finding's full causal chain holds:\\n\\n1. A filter is committed (not cleared) via the Enter path. internal/tui/tui.go:264-267 updateFiltering \\\"enter\\\" sets m.filtering=false and m.filter.Blur() but intentionally does NOT clear the value; only the \\\"esc\\\" path (lines 258-262) calls m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\"). So m.filter.Value() can be non-empty while m.filtering==false.\\n\\n2. With m.filtering==false, the s-key is reachable. The main key switch returns early only for m.filtering (147-149) and m.searching (150-152); the Search binding is matched at tui.go:164-170, which sets m.searching/Focus and clears only m.search (not m.filter). runSearchCmd (297-306) merely calls fn. So the committed filter survives to the result handler untouched.\\n\\n3. The searchResultMsg handler (tui.go:125-142) switches m.tab=TabSearch, resets columns/cursor, and calls m.rebuildVisible() (line 141) but never clears m.filter — unlike switchTab (tui.go:213) which does m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\").\\n\\n4. rebuildVisible (tui.go:397-410) reads q = strings.ToLower(strings.TrimSpace(m.filter.Value())) and keeps only indices where strings.Contains(m.snap.filterKey(m.tab, i), q). filterKey has a real TabSearch case (viewmodels.go:237-239), so a stale filter silently drops every search hit whose Source/Text/Path/Heading doesn't also contain the filter text.\\n\\n5. The tab bar count disagrees: renderTabs (tui.go:466-481) prints \\\"Search (%d)\\\" using m.snap.count(t), and count(TabSearch) returns len(s.Search) (viewmodels.go:210-211) — the UNfiltered total. So the header shows e.g. Search (2) while the visible list is empty, and real results vanish with no indication of why.\\n\\nThis is a reachable, non-contrived user flow (commit a filter on Facts with '/'+Enter, then press 's', search) that silently hides correct results and produces a count/visible mismatch. The suggested fix is correct and minimal: add m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\") immediately before m.rebuildVisible() in the searchResultMsg case (around tui.go:136-141), mirroring switchTab. A maintainer would fix this.\",\n21\t          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n22\t        }\n23\t      },\n24\t      {\n25\t        \"title\": \"repoDirName can return \\\"..\\\" or \\\".\\\", making dest the parent/current dir and fetching into an unintended repo\",\n26\t        \"file\": \"internal/cli/add.go\",\n27\t        \"line\": \"106-133, 50, 63\",\n28\t        \"severity\": \"bug\",\n29\t        \"detail\": \"repoDirName splits the URL path on '/' and joins the last two non-empty segments with '_', but it does not reject '.' or '..' segments. For a crafted URL whose path tail is a single dot-dot segment (e.g. `entire brain add https://h/..` or scp-form `git@h:..`), repoDirName returns \\\"..\\\"; `https://h/.` returns \\\".\\\". Then dest = filepath.Join(flags.dir, name): with the default --dir=\\\".\\\" this yields dest=\\\"..\\\" (the parent directory) or \\\".\\\" (the current directory). isGitCheckout(dest) is then evaluated against that real directory: if the parent/cwd is itself a git checkout, add prints \\\"already present, skipping\\\" and proceeds to run `git fetch --quiet origin refs/heads/entire/*:refs/heads/entire/*` *inside the user's own surrounding repository* (add.go:63), creating refs/heads/entire/* branches there from whatever that repo's origin exposes — a mutation of an unintended repo. If it is not a checkout, `git clone <url> ..` fails on a non-empty dest, which is merely a confusing error. Verified empirically: repoDirName(\\\"https://h/..\\\")==\\\"..\\\", repoDirName(\\\"https://h/.\\\")==\\\".\\\". Multi-segment '..' (e.g. https://h/a/..) is neutralized because the '/' is replaced by '_' (yields \\\"a_..\\\"), so only a trailing single-dot/dot-dot path segment triggers it. Low probability (requires a malformed URL) but a real input-validation gap that can write into a repo the user did not name.\",\n30\t        \"suggested_fix\": \"After deriving name, reject reserved/traversal names before computing dest: e.g. `if name == \\\".\\\" || name == \\\"..\\\" || strings.ContainsAny(name, \\\"/\\\\\\\\\\\") { return fmt.Errorf(\\\"invalid_repo_url: cannot derive a safe directory name from %q\\\", repoURL) }`. Equivalently, validate that filepath.Clean(dest) stays within flags.dir (dest != flags.dir and dest is not an ancestor of flags.dir) before the isGitCheckout/clone/fetch steps.\",\n31\t        \"dimension\": \"add-cmd\",\n32\t        \"verdict\": {\n33\t          \"real\": true,\n34\t          \"reasoning\": \"Confirmed by reading the code and empirically running repoDirName. The finding is accurate.\\n\\nTrace (internal/cli/add.go):\\n- Line 30-33: cobra Args is only ExactArgs(1); no URL validation. args[0] is passed straight to runBrainAdd.\\n- Line 46-49: the only guard is `name == \\\"\\\"`. repoDirName never returns \\\".\\\" or \\\"..\\\" as empty, so they pass.\\n- repoDirName (106-133): splits the post-host path on '/', drops empty segments, and for a single segment returns it verbatim with no reserved-name check. Verified empirically (compiled+ran the exact function):\\n  repoDirName(\\\"https://h/..\\\") == \\\"..\\\"  and  repoDirName(\\\"https://h/.\\\") == \\\".\\\"  (also git@h:.. and git@h:.). Multi-segment \\\"https://h/a/..\\\" correctly neutralizes to \\\"a_..\\\" because '/' becomes '_', so only a trailing single dot/dot-dot segment triggers it — exactly as the finding states.\\n- Line 50: dest = filepath.Join(flags.dir, name). With default --dir=\\\".\\\", filepath.Join(\\\".\\\",\\\"..\\\")==\\\"..\\\", filepath.Join(\\\".\\\",\\\".\\\")==\\\".\\\".\\n- Line 53: isGitCheckout(dest) checks os.Stat(dest/.git). If the user is inside a git repo (the common case), \\\"..\\\" and/or \\\".\\\" resolve to that real checkout, so this returns true and the clone is skipped (\\\"already present, skipping\\\").\\n- Line 63: git fetch is then run with dir=dest (runner.go:18-22 sets cmd.Dir=dir), i.e. inside the user's own surrounding repo. The refspec refs/heads/entire/*:refs/heads/entire/* writes into local refs/heads/entire/* of that repo, pulling from that repo's own `origin` (repoURL is not passed to fetch). This is a real state mutation of a repository the user never named, in a tool otherwise intended to be non-destructive toward the user's environment.\\n\\nIf the parent/cwd is not a git checkout, the clone instead runs `git clone <url> ..`/`.` into a non-empty dir and fails with a confusing error — the lesser case the finding also acknowledges.\\n\\nThis is a genuine input-validation gap with a surprising, undesirable side effect (writing branches into an unintended repo). It requires a malformed/typo'd URL (not attacker-controlled, so robustness rather than security-critical), which is why this is a real-but-not-high-severity bug. The suggested fix — rejecting name==\\\".\\\" || name==\\\"..\\\" || name containing path separators right after deriving name (or validating filepath.Clean(dest) stays within flags.dir) — is correct, minimal, and would close the gap cleanly.\",\n35\t          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n36\t        }\n37\t      },\n38\t      {\n39\t        \"title\": \"repoURL is passed to git clone without a leading-dash guard / `--` separator\",\n40\t        \"file\": \"internal/cli/add.go\",\n41\t        \"line\": \"57\",\n42\t        \"severity\": \"minor\",\n43\t        \"detail\": \"opts.Runner.Run(ctx, \\\"\\\", \\\"git\\\", \\\"clone\\\", \\\"--quiet\\\", repoURL, dest) places the user-controlled repoURL as a positional that git parses for options. A repoURL beginning with '-' (e.g. `--upload-pack=...`, `-c`, `--config`) is interpreted by git as a flag rather than a URL. This is the standard git-clone argument-injection class. In this specific call it does not reach code execution: there is only one user-controlled positional, so when it is consumed as a flag `dest` (a nonexistent local path) becomes the repository argument and git errors out before any transport is established; the `ext::` transport is also blocked by git's default protocol.ext.allow=user (both confirmed empirically with git 2.47). So this is a latent hardening gap, not an exploitable RCE today — but it relies on incidental call ordering. repoURL is correctly passed as an exec arg (no shell), so there is no shell-injection.\",\n44\t        \"suggested_fix\": \"Add an end-of-options separator so the URL can never be parsed as a flag: `opts.Runner.Run(ctx, \\\"\\\", \\\"git\\\", \\\"clone\\\", \\\"--quiet\\\", \\\"--\\\", repoURL, dest)`, and/or reject repoURL values beginning with '-' up front in runBrainAdd.\",\n45\t        \"dimension\": \"add-cmd\",\n46\t        \"verdict\": {\n47\t          \"real\": true,\n48\t          \"reasoning\": \"Confirmed against the code. internal/cli/add.go:57 calls opts.Runner.Run(ctx, \\\"\\\", \\\"git\\\", \\\"clone\\\", \\\"--quiet\\\", repoURL, dest), and ExecRunner.Run (runner.go:18-19) does exec.CommandContext(ctx, name, args...) — so repoURL is passed as a raw argv element with no shell (no shell-injection, as the finding correctly notes), but also with no end-of-options guard. repoURL is user-controlled (args[0] from runBrainAdd, add.go:41/32). A value beginning with '-' (e.g. --upload-pack=..., -c, --config) is parsed by git as an option rather than a positional URL. repoDirName does not reject leading dashes, so such a value flows through to the clone call. This is the standard git-clone argument-injection hardening class.\\n\\nThe finding's own assessment is accurate and honest: it is NOT exploitable as RCE today — there is a single user-controlled positional, so when it is consumed as a flag, the nonexistent dest path becomes the repository argument and git errors out before transport, and ext:: is blocked by git's default protocol.ext.allow=user. So today the only observable impact is a confusing git error (instead of a clean invalid_repo_url message) and reliance on incidental argument ordering for safety rather than an explicit guard. That makes it a genuine, minor, latent-hardening defect — real but appropriately scoped to severity=minor. A maintainer doing CLI-arg-to-git plumbing would apply the trivial, zero-downside fix. It is evidence-grounded at a specific file:line with a concrete, idiomatic fix, and is not speculative or purely stylistic.\\n\\nSuggested fix (as proposed): insert the end-of-options separator — opts.Runner.Run(ctx, \\\"\\\", \\\"git\\\", \\\"clone\\\", \\\"--quiet\\\", \\\"--\\\", repoURL, dest) — and/or reject repoURL values beginning with '-' up front in runBrainAdd (add.go:46-49 area).\",\n49\t          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n50\t        }\n51\t      },\n52\t      {\n53\t        \"title\": \"Search 'open source' on fact-source results points at a bogus in-brain path (taxonomy labels treated as a file path)\",\n54\t        \"file\": \"internal/cli/dash.go\",\n55\t        \"line\": \"109-112\",\n56\t        \"severity\": \"bug\",\n57\t        \"detail\": \"brainSearchFunc maps unifiedResult.Path directly into SearchResult.OpenPath via brainRelPath for every source. That is correct for `history` and `doc` results, whose Path is a genuine brain-relative file path (history.go:74 record Path e.g. \\\"sessions/main/review-env.jsonl\\\"; doc.go:44 \\\"brain-relative markdown path\\\"). It is WRONG for `fact` results: factsToUnified (retrieve.go:275) sets Path = strings.Join(f.Paths, \\\",\\\"), and f.Paths are taxonomy labels / topic strings, not filesystem paths (facts.go:87: \\\"1-2 taxonomy paths (topic label)\\\"). So for a fact hit, brainRelPath(brainDir, \\\"arch/storage\\\") resolves to <brainDir>/arch/storage — a path that does not exist in the brain — and for a two-path fact the comma-joined value \\\"arch/storage,perf/io\\\" yields the nonsense target <brainDir>/arch/storage,perf/io. brainRelPath's containment still holds (the result never escapes brainDir, so this is not an egress/security issue), but renderSearchResult (detail.go:93) then shows the OSC8 link + \\\"(press o)\\\" affordance, and pressing `o` runs openCmd on a non-existent path — a silent no-op / wrong-target. This diverges from the Facts TAB, which correctly derives Source from f.Provenance[0].Transcript (a real brain-relative transcript) via brainRelPath (dash.go:305-307). The existing tui_test only exercises a history-style result (tui_test.go:239 Path \\\"sessions/x.jsonl\\\"), so this fact-path case is untested.\",\n58\t        \"suggested_fix\": \"Do not treat a fact result's Path as a file path. Either (a) gate OpenPath on source: only set sr.OpenPath/sr.OpenLine via brainRelPath when r.Source is \\\"history\\\" or \\\"doc\\\" (leaving facts with no open target, matching that facts' Path is a topic label, not a file); or (b) for fact results resolve the open target from the fact's provenance transcript the way dashFactViews does, rather than from r.Path. Option (a) is the minimal, consistent fix: `if r.Path != \\\"\\\" && (r.Source == \\\"history\\\" || r.Source == \\\"doc\\\") { sr.OpenPath = brainRelPath(brainDir, r.Path); sr.OpenLine = r.Line }`. Add a tui/cli test covering a fact-source SearchResult to lock the behavior.\",\n59\t        \"dimension\": \"regress-integration\",\n60\t        \"verdict\": {\n61\t          \"real\": true,\n62\t          \"reasoning\": \"Confirmed genuine after reading the full code path.\\n\\nEvidence chain for a `fact`-source search hit:\\n- retrieve.go:275 `factsToUnified`: `unifiedResult{Source: \\\"fact\\\", ..., Path: strings.Join(f.Paths, \\\",\\\"), ...}`. So a fact's Path is its taxonomy labels, not a file path.\\n- facts.go:87 documents `Paths []string` as \\\"1-2 taxonomy paths (topic label)\\\"; facts.go:38/150 `factPathPattern = ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*(\\\\.[a-z0-9_]+){2}$` and `validFactPath` enforce a three-level dotted label (e.g. \\\"arch.storage.layout\\\") — never a filesystem path. The two-path case comma-joins to e.g. \\\"arch.storage.layout,perf.io.batching\\\".\\n- dash.go:109-111 `brainSearchFunc`: `if r.Path != \\\"\\\" { sr.OpenPath = brainRelPath(brainDir, r.Path); sr.OpenLine = r.Line }` — applied unconditionally to every source, so a fact's taxonomy-label Path is treated as a brain-relative file path.\\n- dash.go:509-522 `brainRelPath`: \\\"arch.storage.layout\\\" is not abs and not \\\"..\\\", so it returns `<brainDir>/arch.storage.layout` (containment holds — no escape/egress — but the path does not exist). The comma value resolves to `<brainDir>/arch.storage.layout,perf.io.batching`, equally nonexistent.\\n- detail.go:93 `renderSearchResult` -> detail.go:411-422 `writeOpenHint`: since OpenPath != \\\"\\\", it renders the OSC8 \\\"↗ open source\\\" hyperlink plus the \\\"(press o)\\\" affordance.\\n- viewmodels.go:265-268 `openTarget` for TabSearch returns `s.Search[i].OpenPath`; tui.go:369-373 `openSelected` -> openCmd runs the OS opener (tui.go:378-389 `open`/`xdg-open`/`rundll32`) on that bogus path — a silent no-op / wrong target.\\n\\nDivergence confirmed: the Facts TAB (dash.go:305-307) correctly derives its open Source from `f.Provenance[0].Transcript` (a real brain-relative transcript) via brainRelPath, while Search derives it from r.Path. So the same fact opens a real transcript from the Facts tab but a nonexistent `<brainDir>/<taxonomy-label>` from Search. history/doc results are unaffected: historyToUnified (retrieve.go:283) and docToUnified (retrieve.go:289) set Path to genuine brain-relative file paths.\\n\\nImpact: a user-facing misleading affordance (open link + press-o hint) that targets a path that doesn't exist, only for fact-source search hits. Not a security/egress issue (brainRelPath containment holds) but a real correctness/UX defect a maintainer would fix. The cited file:line (dash.go:109-112), the structs, and the comparison to dash.go:305-307 are all accurate.\\n\\nSuggested fix matches option (a): gate the open target on source so facts get no open affordance:\\n`if r.Path != \\\"\\\" && (r.Source == \\\"history\\\" || r.Source == \\\"doc\\\") { sr.OpenPath = brainRelPath(brainDir, r.Path); sr.OpenLine = r.Line }`\\n(Alternatively, for facts resolve the open target from the fact's provenance transcript as dashFactViews does.) Add a tui/cli test covering a fact-source SearchResult to lock the behavior; the existing tui_test only exercises a history-style Path.\",\n63\t          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n64\t        }\n65\t      }\n66\t    ],\n67\t    \"raisedCount\": 6,\n68\t    \"confirmedCount\": 4\n69\t  }\n70\t}"}]},"uuid":"cfac828f-8d4f-4097-935d-d03d848714bd","timestamp":"2026-06-17T03:45:20.632Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-Documents-Coding-entire-brain/4e09703c-666f-441f-9b82-52efaa7da599/tasks/wgrbwnsmc.output","content":"{\n  \"summary\": \"Adversarial audit of the new search-in-TUI + `entire brain add` + prior-fix regressions\",\n  \"agentCount\": 9,\n  \"logs\": [\n    \"v2 audit: 3 dimensions over search + add + prior-fix regressions\",\n    \"raised 6, confirmed 4\"\n  ],\n  \"result\": {\n    \"confirmed\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"A committed filter from the prior tab silently hides in-dashboard search results\",\n        \"file\": \"internal/tui/tui.go\",\n        \"line\": \"125-142\",\n        \"severity\": \"bug\",\n        \"detail\": \"The searchResultMsg handler switches to TabSearch and calls rebuildVisible() but never clears m.filter, unlike switchTab() (line 213) which does m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\"). A filter is committed (not just typed) by pressing Enter in updateFiltering (lines 264-267), which sets m.filtering=false while deliberately keeping m.filter.Value() so the filter stays applied. Because the main key switch only gates / and s behind !m.filtering (lines 147-152), the user can legitimately reach search with a non-empty committed filter. Sequence: on Facts, press '/', type a string, Enter (filter committed); then press 's', type a query, Enter. When searchResultMsg lands, rebuildVisible() (line 141 -> line 398) applies q = m.filter.Value() over the TabSearch results via filterKey(TabSearch, i), silently dropping any search hit that doesn't also contain the stale filter text. The tab bar still shows Search (N) from m.snap.count(TabSearch) (the unfiltered total, tui.go:473 / viewmodels.go:210-211), so the count and the visible list disagree and real results vanish. I confirmed this with a focused test: 2 search results existed, a stale committed filter 'zzznomatch' made visible=0 while the tab bar reported Search (2).\",\n        \"suggested_fix\": \"In the searchResultMsg case (around line 136-141), clear the carried-over filter before rebuilding, mirroring switchTab: add m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\") (and, for consistency, m.filtering = false is already implied) immediately before m.rebuildVisible() so search results are shown unfiltered and the Search (N) count matches the visible rows.\",\n        \"dimension\": \"search-fsm\",\n        \"verdict\": {\n          \"real\": true,\n          \"reasoning\": \"Confirmed by reading the code. The finding's full causal chain holds:\\n\\n1. A filter is committed (not cleared) via the Enter path. internal/tui/tui.go:264-267 updateFiltering \\\"enter\\\" sets m.filtering=false and m.filter.Blur() but intentionally does NOT clear the value; only the \\\"esc\\\" path (lines 258-262) calls m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\"). So m.filter.Value() can be non-empty while m.filtering==false.\\n\\n2. With m.filtering==false, the s-key is reachable. The main key switch returns early only for m.filtering (147-149) and m.searching (150-152); the Search binding is matched at tui.go:164-170, which sets m.searching/Focus and clears only m.search (not m.filter). runSearchCmd (297-306) merely calls fn. So the committed filter survives to the result handler untouched.\\n\\n3. The searchResultMsg handler (tui.go:125-142) switches m.tab=TabSearch, resets columns/cursor, and calls m.rebuildVisible() (line 141) but never clears m.filter — unlike switchTab (tui.go:213) which does m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\").\\n\\n4. rebuildVisible (tui.go:397-410) reads q = strings.ToLower(strings.TrimSpace(m.filter.Value())) and keeps only indices where strings.Contains(m.snap.filterKey(m.tab, i), q). filterKey has a real TabSearch case (viewmodels.go:237-239), so a stale filter silently drops every search hit whose Source/Text/Path/Heading doesn't also contain the filter text.\\n\\n5. The tab bar count disagrees: renderTabs (tui.go:466-481) prints \\\"Search (%d)\\\" using m.snap.count(t), and count(TabSearch) returns len(s.Search) (viewmodels.go:210-211) — the UNfiltered total. So the header shows e.g. Search (2) while the visible list is empty, and real results vanish with no indication of why.\\n\\nThis is a reachable, non-contrived user flow (commit a filter on Facts with '/'+Enter, then press 's', search) that silently hides correct results and produces a count/visible mismatch. The suggested fix is correct and minimal: add m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\") immediately before m.rebuildVisible() in the searchResultMsg case (around tui.go:136-141), mirroring switchTab. A maintainer would fix this.\",\n          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"repoDirName can return \\\"..\\\" or \\\".\\\", making dest the parent/current dir and fetching into an unintended repo\",\n        \"file\": \"internal/cli/add.go\",\n        \"line\": \"106-133, 50, 63\",\n        \"severity\": \"bug\",\n        \"detail\": \"repoDirName splits the URL path on '/' and joins the last two non-empty segments with '_', but it does not reject '.' or '..' segments. For a crafted URL whose path tail is a single dot-dot segment (e.g. `entire brain add https://h/..` or scp-form `git@h:..`), repoDirName returns \\\"..\\\"; `https://h/.` returns \\\".\\\". Then dest = filepath.Join(flags.dir, name): with the default --dir=\\\".\\\" this yields dest=\\\"..\\\" (the parent directory) or \\\".\\\" (the current directory). isGitCheckout(dest) is then evaluated against that real directory: if the parent/cwd is itself a git checkout, add prints \\\"already present, skipping\\\" and proceeds to run `git fetch --quiet origin refs/heads/entire/*:refs/heads/entire/*` *inside the user's own surrounding repository* (add.go:63), creating refs/heads/entire/* branches there from whatever that repo's origin exposes — a mutation of an unintended repo. If it is not a checkout, `git clone <url> ..` fails on a non-empty dest, which is merely a confusing error. Verified empirically: repoDirName(\\\"https://h/..\\\")==\\\"..\\\", repoDirName(\\\"https://h/.\\\")==\\\".\\\". Multi-segment '..' (e.g. https://h/a/..) is neutralized because the '/' is replaced by '_' (yields \\\"a_..\\\"), so only a trailing single-dot/dot-dot path segment triggers it. Low probability (requires a malformed URL) but a real input-validation gap that can write into a repo the user did not name.\",\n        \"suggested_fix\": \"After deriving name, reject reserved/traversal names before computing dest: e.g. `if name == \\\".\\\" || name == \\\"..\\\" || strings.ContainsAny(name, \\\"/\\\\\\\\\\\") { return fmt.Errorf(\\\"invalid_repo_url: cannot derive a safe directory name from %q\\\", repoURL) }`. Equivalently, validate that filepath.Clean(dest) stays within flags.dir (dest != flags.dir and dest is not an ancestor of flags.dir) before the isGitCheckout/clone/fetch steps.\",\n        \"dimension\": \"add-cmd\",\n        \"verdict\": {\n          \"real\": true,\n          \"reasoning\": \"Confirmed by reading the code and empirically running repoDirName. The finding is accurate.\\n\\nTrace (internal/cli/add.go):\\n- Line 30-33: cobra Args is only ExactArgs(1); no URL validation. args[0] is passed straight to runBrainAdd.\\n- Line 46-49: the only guard is `name == \\\"\\\"`. repoDirName never returns \\\".\\\" or \\\"..\\\" as empty, so they pass.\\n- repoDirName (106-133): splits the post-host path on '/', drops empty segments, and for a single segment returns it verbatim with no reserved-name check. Verified empirically (compiled+ran the exact function):\\n  repoDirName(\\\"https://h/..\\\") == \\\"..\\\"  and  repoDirName(\\\"https://h/.\\\") == \\\".\\\"  (also git@h:.. and git@h:.). Multi-segment \\\"https://h/a/..\\\" correctly neutralizes to \\\"a_..\\\" because '/' becomes '_', so only a trailing single dot/dot-dot segment triggers it — exactly as the finding states.\\n- Line 50: dest = filepath.Join(flags.dir, name). With default --dir=\\\".\\\", filepath.Join(\\\".\\\",\\\"..\\\")==\\\"..\\\", filepath.Join(\\\".\\\",\\\".\\\")==\\\".\\\".\\n- Line 53: isGitCheckout(dest) checks os.Stat(dest/.git). If the user is inside a git repo (the common case), \\\"..\\\" and/or \\\".\\\" resolve to that real checkout, so this returns true and the clone is skipped (\\\"already present, skipping\\\").\\n- Line 63: git fetch is then run with dir=dest (runner.go:18-22 sets cmd.Dir=dir), i.e. inside the user's own surrounding repo. The refspec refs/heads/entire/*:refs/heads/entire/* writes into local refs/heads/entire/* of that repo, pulling from that repo's own `origin` (repoURL is not passed to fetch). This is a real state mutation of a repository the user never named, in a tool otherwise intended to be non-destructive toward the user's environment.\\n\\nIf the parent/cwd is not a git checkout, the clone instead runs `git clone <url> ..`/`.` into a non-empty dir and fails with a confusing error — the lesser case the finding also acknowledges.\\n\\nThis is a genuine input-validation gap with a surprising, undesirable side effect (writing branches into an unintended repo). It requires a malformed/typo'd URL (not attacker-controlled, so robustness rather than security-critical), which is why this is a real-but-not-high-severity bug. The suggested fix — rejecting name==\\\".\\\" || name==\\\"..\\\" || name containing path separators right after deriving name (or validating filepath.Clean(dest) stays within flags.dir) — is correct, minimal, and would close the gap cleanly.\",\n          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"repoURL is passed to git clone without a leading-dash guard / `--` separator\",\n        \"file\": \"internal/cli/add.go\",\n        \"line\": \"57\",\n        \"severity\": \"minor\",\n        \"detail\": \"opts.Runner.Run(ctx, \\\"\\\", \\\"git\\\", \\\"clone\\\", \\\"--quiet\\\", repoURL, dest) places the user-controlled repoURL as a positional that git parses for options. A repoURL beginning with '-' (e.g. `--upload-pack=...`, `-c`, `--config`) is interpreted by git as a flag rather than a URL. This is the standard git-clone argument-injection class. In this specific call it does not reach code execution: there is only one user-controlled positional, so when it is consumed as a flag `dest` (a nonexistent local path) becomes the repository argument and git errors out before any transport is established; the `ext::` transport is also blocked by git's default protocol.ext.allow=user (both confirmed empirically with git 2.47). So this is a latent hardening gap, not an exploitable RCE today — but it relies on incidental call ordering. repoURL is correctly passed as an exec arg (no shell), so there is no shell-injection.\",\n        \"suggested_fix\": \"Add an end-of-options separator so the URL can never be parsed as a flag: `opts.Runner.Run(ctx, \\\"\\\", \\\"git\\\", \\\"clone\\\", \\\"--quiet\\\", \\\"--\\\", repoURL, dest)`, and/or reject repoURL values beginning with '-' up front in runBrainAdd.\",\n        \"dimension\": \"add-cmd\",\n        \"verdict\": {\n          \"real\": true,\n          \"reasoning\": \"Confirmed against the code. internal/cli/add.go:57 calls opts.Runner.Run(ctx, \\\"\\\", \\\"git\\\", \\\"clone\\\", \\\"--quiet\\\", repoURL, dest), and ExecRunner.Run (runner.go:18-19) does exec.CommandContext(ctx, name, args...) — so repoURL is passed as a raw argv element with no shell (no shell-injection, as the finding correctly notes), but also with no end-of-options guard. repoURL is user-controlled (args[0] from runBrainAdd, add.go:41/32). A value beginning with '-' (e.g. --upload-pack=..., -c, --config) is parsed by git as an option rather than a positional URL. repoDirName does not reject leading dashes, so such a value flows through to the clone call. This is the standard git-clone argument-injection hardening class.\\n\\nThe finding's own assessment is accurate and honest: it is NOT exploitable as RCE today — there is a single user-controlled positional, so when it is consumed as a flag, the nonexistent dest path becomes the repository argument and git errors out before transport, and ext:: is blocked by git's default protocol.ext.allow=user. So today the only observable impact is a confusing git error (instead of a clean invalid_repo_url message) and reliance on incidental argument ordering for safety rather than an explicit guard. That makes it a genuine, minor, latent-hardening defect — real but appropriately scoped to severity=minor. A maintainer doing CLI-arg-to-git plumbing would apply the trivial, zero-downside fix. It is evidence-grounded at a specific file:line with a concrete, idiomatic fix, and is not speculative or purely stylistic.\\n\\nSuggested fix (as proposed): insert the end-of-options separator — opts.Runner.Run(ctx, \\\"\\\", \\\"git\\\", \\\"clone\\\", \\\"--quiet\\\", \\\"--\\\", repoURL, dest) — and/or reject repoURL values beginning with '-' up front in runBrainAdd (add.go:46-49 area).\",\n          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Search 'open source' on fact-source results points at a bogus in-brain path (taxonomy labels treated as a file path)\",\n        \"file\": \"internal/cli/dash.go\",\n        \"line\": \"109-112\",\n        \"severity\": \"bug\",\n        \"detail\": \"brainSearchFunc maps unifiedResult.Path directly into SearchResult.OpenPath via brainRelPath for every source. That is correct for `history` and `doc` results, whose Path is a genuine brain-relative file path (history.go:74 record Path e.g. \\\"sessions/main/review-env.jsonl\\\"; doc.go:44 \\\"brain-relative markdown path\\\"). It is WRONG for `fact` results: factsToUnified (retrieve.go:275) sets Path = strings.Join(f.Paths, \\\",\\\"), and f.Paths are taxonomy labels / topic strings, not filesystem paths (facts.go:87: \\\"1-2 taxonomy paths (topic label)\\\"). So for a fact hit, brainRelPath(brainDir, \\\"arch/storage\\\") resolves to <brainDir>/arch/storage — a path that does not exist in the brain — and for a two-path fact the comma-joined value \\\"arch/storage,perf/io\\\" yields the nonsense target <brainDir>/arch/storage,perf/io. brainRelPath's containment still holds (the result never escapes brainDir, so this is not an egress/security issue), but renderSearchResult (detail.go:93) then shows the OSC8 link + \\\"(press o)\\\" affordance, and pressing `o` runs openCmd on a non-existent path — a silent no-op / wrong-target. This diverges from the Facts TAB, which correctly derives Source from f.Provenance[0].Transcript (a real brain-relative transcript) via brainRelPath (dash.go:305-307). The existing tui_test only exercises a history-style result (tui_test.go:239 Path \\\"sessions/x.jsonl\\\"), so this fact-path case is untested.\",\n        \"suggested_fix\": \"Do not treat a fact result's Path as a file path. Either (a) gate OpenPath on source: only set sr.OpenPath/sr.OpenLine via brainRelPath when r.Source is \\\"history\\\" or \\\"doc\\\" (leaving facts with no open target, matching that facts' Path is a topic label, not a file); or (b) for fact results resolve the open target from the fact's provenance transcript the way dashFactViews does, rather than from r.Path. Option (a) is the minimal, consistent fix: `if r.Path != \\\"\\\" && (r.Source == \\\"history\\\" || r.Source == \\\"doc\\\") { sr.OpenPath = brainRelPath(brainDir, r.Path); sr.OpenLine = r.Line }`. Add a tui/cli test covering a fact-source SearchResult to lock the behavior.\",\n        \"dimension\": \"regress-integration\",\n        \"verdict\": {\n          \"real\": true,\n          \"reasoning\": \"Confirmed genuine after reading the full code path.\\n\\nEvidence chain for a `fact`-source search hit:\\n- retrieve.go:275 `factsToUnified`: `unifiedResult{Source: \\\"fact\\\", ..., Path: strings.Join(f.Paths, \\\",\\\"), ...}`. So a fact's Path is its taxonomy labels, not a file path.\\n- facts.go:87 documents `Paths []string` as \\\"1-2 taxonomy paths (topic label)\\\"; facts.go:38/150 `factPathPattern = ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*(\\\\.[a-z0-9_]+){2}$` and `validFactPath` enforce a three-level dotted label (e.g. \\\"arch.storage.layout\\\") — never a filesystem path. The two-path case comma-joins to e.g. \\\"arch.storage.layout,perf.io.batching\\\".\\n- dash.go:109-111 `brainSearchFunc`: `if r.Path != \\\"\\\" { sr.OpenPath = brainRelPath(brainDir, r.Path); sr.OpenLine = r.Line }` — applied unconditionally to every source, so a fact's taxonomy-label Path is treated as a brain-relative file path.\\n- dash.go:509-522 `brainRelPath`: \\\"arch.storage.layout\\\" is not abs and not \\\"..\\\", so it returns `<brainDir>/arch.storage.layout` (containment holds — no escape/egress — but the path does not exist). The comma value resolves to `<brainDir>/arch.storage.layout,perf.io.batching`, equally nonexistent.\\n- detail.go:93 `renderSearchResult` -> detail.go:411-422 `writeOpenHint`: since OpenPath != \\\"\\\", it renders the OSC8 \\\"↗ open source\\\" hyperlink plus the \\\"(press o)\\\" affordance.\\n- viewmodels.go:265-268 `openTarget` for TabSearch returns `s.Search[i].OpenPath`; tui.go:369-373 `openSelected` -> openCmd runs the OS opener (tui.go:378-389 `open`/`xdg-open`/`rundll32`) on that bogus path — a silent no-op / wrong target.\\n\\nDivergence confirmed: the Facts TAB (dash.go:305-307) correctly derives its open Source from `f.Provenance[0].Transcript` (a real brain-relative transcript) via brainRelPath, while Search derives it from r.Path. So the same fact opens a real transcript from the Facts tab but a nonexistent `<brainDir>/<taxonomy-label>` from Search. history/doc results are unaffected: historyToUnified (retrieve.go:283) and docToUnified (retrieve.go:289) set Path to genuine brain-relative file paths.\\n\\nImpact: a user-facing misleading affordance (open link + press-o hint) that targets a path that doesn't exist, only for fact-source search hits. Not a security/egress issue (brainRelPath containment holds) but a real correctness/UX defect a maintainer would fix. The cited file:line (dash.go:109-112), the structs, and the comparison to dash.go:305-307 are all accurate.\\n\\nSuggested fix matches option (a): gate the open target on source so facts get no open affordance:\\n`if r.Path != \\\"\\\" && (r.Source == \\\"history\\\" || r.Source == \\\"doc\\\") { sr.OpenPath = brainRelPath(brainDir, r.Path); sr.OpenLine = r.Line }`\\n(Alternatively, for facts resolve the open target from the fact's provenance transcript as dashFactViews does.) Add a tui/cli test covering a fact-source SearchResult to lock the behavior; the existing tui_test only exercises a history-style Path.\",\n          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n        }\n      }\n    ],\n    \"raisedCount\": 6,\n    \"confirmedCount\": 4\n  }\n}","numLines":70,"startLine":1,"totalLines":70}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cab550c8-ab2c-4345-80d3-9db30a620b23","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/entire-brain","sessionId":"af861ed9-9e1d-41a7-8400-b53c2995aed8","version":"2.1.177","gitBranch":"claude/brain-dash-tui","slug":"zippy-floating-lovelace"}
